Z||000000||Hank is Here||10-20-2000||03:26 AM||||Okay, but not really, well sorta.........<BR>My turn signal no longer auto returns on left turns but still on right turns, kinda reminds me of like when i drive a large truck. Hopefully it is a  $0.50 spring.    <BR>Please Help<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||10-20-2000||12:16 PM||||I actually <B>did</B> repair a turn-signal- canceller with a spring from hardware store, for like 38 cents!  If you have ever pulled a steering wheel and are somewhat familiar with what's in the column, it's pretty easy.  It <B>does</B> take time, though, and some patience    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  <P>After getting the wheel and lock plate off, it was easy to see that one of the two canceller springs was broken.  Removed the pieces of broken spring, took them to the hardware store, and found a new spring of equivalent wire diameter and coil diameter.<P>Back home, I clipped off a coil (or two?) of the new spring, straightened one end and shaped it so that it looked like the other (good) spring.  Installed the homemade part in the column, put it all back together, and was kind of surprised that it worked perfectly    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !<P>Of course, if you can just buy the spring, it will save a little time.  Still, the most time-consuming part for me was taking apart/ reassembling the column.           <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-20-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000002||theogre||10-20-2000||07:49 PM||||If it works one way and not the other you'll likely need to replace a plastic part in the column. It's the piece the signal arm moves to activate the turn lights. Sort of a loop shaped thing right below the lock plate.<P>You can get this in the red HELP! packages from Motormite that are sold at most parts stores. The chains like pep boys and autozone often have them hanging on the sales floor someplace.||||reg||1
